/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:16:# - D16/1: reflection is controlled by the ray/wall color pair
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:18:#   direct wall-color inheritance
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:24:#   - walls are 1-cell or 3-cell axis-aligned monochrome segments
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:26:#   - if current ray color == touched wall color: no reflection; the ray passes
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:28:#   - if current ray color != wall color: reflect exactly as in the base task
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:30:#     color is not the wall color. Instead, it becomes the mixed color
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:36:#   - ray 2 side-touches wall 2 -> no bend, next cells stay 2
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:37:#   - ray 2 side-touches wall 4 -> reflect, and the reflected segment becomes 7
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:38:#   - ray 7 later side-touches wall 4 -> reflect, and the reflected segment
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:41:#   - same-color walls can now be ignored
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:42:#   - different-color reflections no longer inherit the wall color; they switch
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:58:# - Original corner-hit logic assumed every wall blocks and reflects. That fails
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:60:# - Original side-touch logic flipped dr/dc whenever a wall existed on that side.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:62:# - Original bounce recoloring wrote the wall color into the contact cell. That is
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:64:#   recolored to the mixed color, not the wall color.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:66:#   inheritance used the observed wall color directly. The mixed-color rule creates
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:77:# - Arrows, walls, and traced ray cells are kept disjoint in the input.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:78:# - Every planned wall is placed one cell off the diagonal at the intended bounce
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:83:# - Same-color walls are visually "ignored": the ray keeps going straight through
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:84:#   a wall of its own color.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:85:# - Different-color walls bend the ray, but the bent segment is neither the old
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:86:#   ray color nor the wall color. Because only 3 active colors exist, the new
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:94:# - It avoids overlap between arrows, walls, and ray paths, so each object type
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:101:# - Because the same 3 colors repeat across arrows and walls in each grid, a human
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/3/code.py:110:#   immediately collide with unrelated arrows/walls. Generator guarantee: occupied
